                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The combat really starts to drag after a while with them all being the exact same.\r\nThe music is not very interesting but more importantly, not very fittingly used a lot of the time.\r\nThe gearing and crafting is fine, would probably be more interesting if the combat was more interesting.\r\n\r\nAll of that could still be forgiven if the writing and overall story was exceptional, but sadly it ranges between OK to pretty bad. \r\nThe overall story isn't my cup of tea but it could work, if worked on a bit more, but the scene to scene dialogue is either mediocre or downright bad. Some of the characterization is just the most generic writing or downright ripped from other stories, like the elf and dwarf going from enemies to allies being an almost painful lotr copy, and with the elven city name being close to the exact same as Lothlórien that doesn't feel like an accident.\r\nAnother big problem is the way the narrative is told with your character not being \"part of\" the group and the group itself not getting enough interaction or well written dialogue for me to care about them at all.\r\n\r\nOverall I really would like to be able to recommend the game, it being an indie rpg and all, but in the end I was so dejected by the story and tired of the gameplay I turned down from hard to easy and just A-moved thorugh the last 2 levels while speed reading the text. The most emotional I got was being angry at the characters being dumb (Kane speficially), and while that can work in a movie or book, when you're \"playing as\" the characters in a sort-of RPG, it just feels bad.\r\n\r\nGame looks decent, sometimes it even looks great! But I'd be hard pressed to give it more than a 6/10, and there's just too many games out there for that to be a thumbs up.",
